    <DataNode elementId="AT2G20610-MONOMER" textLabel="AT2G20610-MONOMER" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q9SIV0" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="150" centerY="395" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">Three lines of evidence were shown to indicate SUR1 is a C-S lyase in glucosinolate biosynthesis: 1) in vivo feeding experiment to the SUR1 mutant plant with p-hydroxyphenylacetaldoxime accumulated the expected C-S lyase substrate S-(p-hydroxyphenylacetohydroximoyl)-L-cysteine; 2) the SUR1 mutant accumulates much less of all glucosinolates measured, compared with the wild type plants, including methionine-, tryptophan- and phenylalanine-derived glucosinolates. It indicates that SUR1 is involved in the C-S lyase step for all glucosinolate biosynthesis; 3) the enzyme activity was further confirmed with the recombinant protein in E. coli, with a commonly used C-S lyase substrate djenkolic acid.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT2G20610-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Araport" value="AT2G20610.1" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Uniprot" value="Q9SIV0" />
